I've been thinking about writing the stress with an accent instead of a=20
capital letter. The accent need be written only on cmevla. I suggest these=
=20
rules, which aren't complete:

*Syllabic consonants are ignored for counting.
*:m=EDklson
*The stress is by default placed on a certain syllable, counted as if=20
diphthongs were pronounced as one syllable even if written otherwise, and o=
n a=20
certain vowel in that syllable.
*A syllable is counted if it contains a vowel other than "y". A syllable wh=
ich=20
contains "y" and no other vowel is skipped under conditions which aren't=20
exactly clear yet.
*:sautuesrydyfytyn: not clear which syllable gets the stress
*:lylun: the "u" is by default stressed
*:m=E1lybar
*:uili=FDmben
*A string of vowels is broken into syllables starting from the right.

The stressed vowel of a diphthong is like this:
=E1u, =E1i, =E9i, =F3i,
u=E1, u=E9, u=ED, u=F3, u=FA,
i=E1, i=E9, i=ED, i=F3, i=FA,
i=FD, u=FD.
The stressed vowel of a triphthong, if there is any, is the middle vowel.

This is loosely based on the system used in Spanish, which I don't fully kn=
ow.=20
There are (AFAIK) four third-person singular past tense forms of verbs=20
consisting of one consonant followed by "io": dio, fio, rio, vio. Of these,=
=20
"dio" and "vio" are one syllable and "fio" and "rio" are two, accented on "=
o"=20
(this takes some explanation, particularly for "rio", and is irrelevant). T=
he=20
first-person singular present forms of these verbs are "f=EDo" and "r=EDo" =
(the=20
latter is also a noun meaning "river"). Since the default stressed vowel of=
=20
"io" is "o", it has to be written on "f=EDo" and "r=EDo".

Pierre

